{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310604,"nonce":"gXa/DovMJWilsfWfVBIQ37zYJd0=","title":"SUPPLYING an extern's optional/default trailing arg (Sys.command(cmd,args), File.read(path,true)) mis-lowers to wrong arity, fails LLVM ...","message":"Found building the `nue` CLI (2026-07-27). Distinct from\n[[bugs_default_arg_nonzero_funcid_drift]] (which is about OMITTING a default)\nand [[bugs_optional_arg_unfilled_garbage_abi_slot]] (omitted `?opt` read\ngarbage, FIXED): here the argument IS SUPPLIED and codegen breaks.\n\nMINIMAL REPRO — 5 lines, no nue, no toml:\n```haxe\nclass Main {\n    public static function main():Void {\n        Sys.println(\"start\");\n        var a = [\"two-arg-ok\"];\n        var r = Sys.command(\"echo\", a);   // extern: command(cmd:String, ?args:Array\u003cString\u003e)\n        Sys.println(\"rc=\" + r);\n    }\n}\n```\nOutput: `Incorrect number of arguments passed to called function!` then\n`[tier] LLVM upgrade failed: LLVM verification failed for function 'main'`,\nand **the program prints NOTHING AT ALL** — not even the `start` that precedes\nthe call. The one-argument form `Sys.command(\"echo one-arg-ok\")` works and\nreturns 0.\n\nSame shape with a DEFAULT rather than `?`: `File.read(path, true)` where the\nextern is `read(path:String, binary:Bool = true)`. `File.read(path)` is fine.\n\n**Severity is the point:** it is silent. There is no compile error, the tier\nfalls back to Cranelift and \"continues\", and the whole enclosing function\nproduces no output — so it reads as a hang or an empty result, not a bug.\nCost me three wrong diagnoses (buffering, then Sys.exit not flushing) before\ncapturing the exit code.\n\n**Diagnosis trap:** `cmd | grep ... | head` returns the PIPELINE's status, so a\nSIGSEGV (139) reads as a clean exit with no output. Redirect to a file and echo\n`$?` before concluding anything about missing output. See\n[[feedback_debug_at_head_not_timetravel]].\n\nsignature\nThe stdlib-mapping resolver tries arity-matched lookups first and then falls\nback to name-only ones that IGNORE arity. It appears in at least two paths:\n\n- **General static calls — `hir_to_mir.rs:6874-6920`** (this is the one\n  `Sys.command` takes). A four-deep chain: two `find_by_name_and_params`\n  attempts guarded by `if let Some(count) = param_count`, then FOUR\n  arity-blind `find_by_name` fallbacks (underscore name, bare name,\n  qualified-name split, receiver hint).\n- **Property accessors — `hir_to_mir.rs:1509`** (`tls.value` style), same shape:\n  `.find_by_name_and_params(...).or_else(|| find_by_name(...))`.\n\n(An earlier revision of this note cited only 1509 as the cause; that is the\nproperty path and would not resolve `Sys.command`. Same defect, wrong site.)\n\nOn the property path the extern's parameter list is then synthesised from the\nCALL SITE, not from the mapping:\n\n```rust\nlet param_types = vec![ptr_u8.clone(); args.len()];\n```\n\nSo a call whose arity matches NO mapping still resolves by name, and is then\ndeclared with the caller's arity while bound to a native symbol of a different\none. LLVM's verifier rejects the module (\"Incorrect number of arguments\"), the\ntier falls back, and the function silently emits nothing.\n\nThe two stdlib cases are genuine declaration/runtime mismatches:\n- `runtime_mapping.rs:1736` `Sys.command =\u003e haxe_sys_command, params: 1`, but\n  `Sys.hx:154` declares `command(cmd:String, ?args:Array\u003cString\u003e)`. The runtime\n  `haxe_sys_command(cmd: *const HaxeString)` takes ONE arg — **`?args` is simply\n  unimplemented**, and the .hx signature advertises an API that does not exist.\n- `runtime_mapping.rs:1814` `File.read =\u003e file_read_default, params: 1` vs\n  `File.hx:86` `read(path:String, binary:Bool = true)`. The symbol name says it:\n  \"read with the DEFAULT flag\".\n\nUser-defined functions are UNAFFECTED — optional and default args work in all\nfour supplied/omitted combinations. This is purely the stdlib-mapping path.\n\n**Fix direction (per [[feedback_no_silent_dispatch_fallthrough]]: not-found must\nFAIL, never widen):** drop the `.or_else(find_by_name(...))` loosening, and when\na name matches but no arity does, raise a compile ERROR naming class, method,\ncall arity and mapping arity. That converts both cases into a clear diagnostic\ninstead of a program that runs and prints nothing. Separately, either implement\n`?args`/`binary` in the runtime or narrow the .hx signatures to what exists.\n\nWorkaround until fixed: call these externs with the trailing optional/default\nOMITTED, building one shell string where needed (quote args yourself).\n\nTWO MORE defects found in the same sitting:\n- **`GGUFReader` over a TRUNCATED buffer SIGSEGVs instead of throwing.** A\n  cheap header-only read (prefix + grow-on-failure) is therefore impossible to\n  recover with try/catch — the process is already gone. Fixing it means\n  bounds-checking reads against the buffer length in GGUFReader. Until then\n  `nue info` pays a whole-file read (4.4 GB for a 7B) to print a dozen fields.\n- **`new Map\u003cString,Int\u003e()` + exists/set/get crashed** a small histogram loop\n  (~291 iterations, \u003c5 distinct keys). Replaced with parallel arrays. Related:\n  [[bugs_stringmap_null_get]], [[bugs_map_for_in_iterates_keys]].\n\nNot silent after all — it PANICS Cranelift, in the direct-CraneliftBackend path\nthe e2e harness uses (`compiler/examples/test_rayzor_stdlib_e2e.rs`):\n\n    cranelift/codegen/src/machinst/abi.rs:1808\n    assertion `left == right` failed   left: 2   right: 1   (gen_call_args)\n\nNamed it by probing `builder.ins().call(func_ref, \u0026arg_values)` in\ncranelift_backend.rs and printing any site where the arg count differs from the\nCranelift signature:\n\n    in 'addString'   -\u003e extern 'haxe_bytes_of_string': 2 args to a 1-param sig\n    in 'writeString' -\u003e extern 'haxe_bytes_of_string': 2 args to a 1-param sig\n\n`haxe_bytes_of_string(s: *const HaxeString)` takes ONE parameter, and the\nmapping says `params: 1`. Haxe's `Bytes.ofString(s:String, ?encoding:Encoding)`\nhas an optional second, the call site fills it, and the extra argument is\ncarried all the way to codegen.\n\n**WRONG SUSPECTS, ruled out with evidence** — do not re-chase these:\n  - `Sys.exit` is NOT the culprit. It looked like it because a probe test used\n    `trace(x)`, which pulls in addString. Its own MIR is clean: decl params=1,\n    sret=false, ret=Void, cc=C, call nargs=1.\n  - Not `should_add_env` (excluded for externs, `is_extern_func =\n    cfg.blocks.is_empty()`), and not sret (`uses_sret=false`).\n  - The existing `call_mismatch` guard in cranelift_backend.rs does not catch\n    it — that guard compares against the MIR param count on a different branch.\n\nBlast radius: any program whose stdlib pull-in includes `addString` /\n`writeString`, i.e. string concatenation inside `trace`, when compiled through\nthe direct-Cranelift path. `rayzor run` (tiered) survives it, which is why this\nhid for so long.\n\nFIX DIRECTION: stop emitting the defaulted optional argument for a mapped\nextern whose mapping declares fewer parameters — fix it in hir_to_mir at the\ncall-lowering site, NOT by truncating in the backend (a silent truncation is\nexactly the fallback [[feedback_no_silent_dispatch_fallthrough]] bans).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_extern_optional_arg_supplied_breaks_codegen` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}